<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.9" extension="2014-06-09"/>
    <code code="51847-2" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Evaluation + Plan note"/>
    <title>Assessment and Plan</title>
    <text><list listType="ordered"><item><content styleCode="Bold">Preventive health visit completed</content>. Health risk assessment reviewed and a personalized prevention plan made. His main risks are alcohol, now within safer limits, and an overweight BMI; blood pressure is normal on propranolol, lipids give a low 10-year cardiovascular risk, and HbA1c is normal. [S]<list><item>Next preventive visit in one year.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Acute viral upper respiratory infection (J06.9)</content>. Resolved within a week without antibiotics or decongestants. [S]<list><item>No further action.</item></list></item><item><content styleCode="Bold">Essential tremor (G25.0)</content>. Steadier on propranolol 60 mg, without side effects; formal rating at the four-week review next week. [S]<list><item>Continue propranolol.</item></list></item></list><paragraph>Personalized prevention plan. A written copy was given to the patient. [S]</paragraph><table><thead><tr><th>Service</th><th>Status</th><th>Detail</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td>Influenza vaccine, this season</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Given in the autumn</td></tr><tr><td>COVID-19 vaccine, this season</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Given in the autumn</td></tr><tr><td>Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PCV20 given today; recommended for all adults aged 50 and over</td></tr><tr><td>Recombinant zoster vaccine</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Dose 1 given today; dose 2 due in two to six months</td></tr><tr><td>Tetanus booster</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Tdap seven and a half years ago; next due in about two years</td></tr><tr><td>Hepatitis B vaccine</td><td>Up to date</td><td>Series completed in his thirties</td></tr><tr><td>Infection and fever precautions</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Hand washing and staying home when febrile in the winter season; drink fluids and avoid alcohol when unwell; call for a fever above 38.5 C lasting more than three days, a fever with breathlessness, chest pain, confusion, a stiff neck, or a rash, or any fever after a wound or bite; vaccines kept up to date</td></tr><tr><td>Colorectal cancer screening</td><td>Ordered</td><td>Never screened; he chose a yearly fecal immunochemical test over colonoscopy; kit given today</td></tr><tr><td>Depression screening</td><td>Completed today</td><td>PHQ-2 score 0</td></tr><tr><td>Alcohol misuse screening and counseling</td><td>Completed today</td><td>AUDIT 12 at intake with a brief intervention; now about 10 drinks a week, no more than three on any day; GGT to be rechecked at the next tremor visit but one</td></tr><tr><td>Blood pressure screening</td><td>Completed today</td><td>122/78 mmHg on propranolol; 134/84 mmHg before it</td></tr><tr><td>Lipid screening and cardiovascular risk</td><td>Completed today</td><td>Total cholesterol 204 mg/dL, HDL 52 mg/dL, LDL 128 mg/dL, triglycerides 122 mg/dL; never smoker; estimated 10-year risk below 5 percent, so no statin; diet and activity advice</td></tr><tr><td>Diabetes screening</td><td>Completed today</td><td>HbA1c 5.4% (screened because he is overweight)</td></tr><tr><td>HIV and hepatitis C screening</td><td>Up to date</td><td>One-time tests negative in his forties</td></tr><tr><td>Prostate cancer screening</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>A shared decision about PSA screening is offered from 55</td></tr><tr><td>Lung cancer and abdominal aortic aneurysm screening</td><td>Not indicated</td><td>Never smoker</td></tr><tr><td>Hearing and vision</td><td>Up to date</td><td>No concerns; eye examination two years ago</td></tr></tbody></table><paragraph>Immunizations given today: Pneumococcal conjugate vaccine PCV20; Recombinant zoster vaccine, dose 1 of 2 [S]</paragraph><paragraph>Guideline basis: Unhealthy Alcohol Use in Adolescents and Adults: Screening and Behavioral Counseling Interventions (2018); Helping Patients Who Drink Too Much: A Clinician&apos;s Guide (2005); Medicare Preventive Services reference: visit templates, screening cascades, and documentation elements (2026); Adult Immunization Schedule by Age (2026); Expanded Recommendations for Use of Pneumococcal Conjugate Vaccines Among Adults Aged ≥50 Years: Recommendations of the Advisory Committee on Immunization Practices (2024); Colorectal Cancer: Screening (2021); Depression and Suicide Risk in Adults: Screening (2023); Statin Use for the Primary Prevention of Cardiovascular Disease in Adults: Preventive Medication (2022); Prostate Cancer: Screening (2018); Hypertension in Adults: Screening (2021)</paragraph><paragraph>Follow-up: Four-week tremor review next week as planned. Second zoster dose in two to six months. Fecal immunochemical test kit to be returned within two weeks. Next scheduled visit 2026-02-23: four-week review of propranolol: tremor rating, pulse and blood pressure, drinking, and occupational therapy. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>

<component>
  <section>
    <templateId root="2.16.840.1.113883.10.20.22.2.21" extension="2015-08-01"/>
    <code code="42348-3" codeSystem="2.16.840.1.113883.6.1" displayName="Advance directives"/>
    <title>Advance Care Planning</title>
    <text><paragraph>Voluntary advance care planning discussion of about 20 minutes, with his consent, prompted by his preparing a will. In clinical terms: he named a close friend from the town as his health care agent and will complete the state directive form, which the clinic will scan into his chart; a copy can sit with his other papers. He values being able to think, read, and write; he would want full treatment for illnesses he could recover from, including hospital care for a serious fever or infection, and he would not want prolonged life support if he could not expect to recover his mind. He asked how his tremor might progress; it usually progresses slowly and does not shorten life. He was reassured that a shaky signature does not reflect his capacity, and that he can take his propranolol in the morning before signing papers. [S]</paragraph></text>

  </section>
</component>
    </structuredBody>
  </component>
</ClinicalDocument>
